# security.generate.stepwise

## How to behave

- Work through findings one at a time, in severity order: **Critical → High → Medium → Low → Informational**
- Before applying any fix:
  - Present the finding (severity, title, location, impact)
  - Describe the intended fix and any trade-offs
  - Stop and wait for explicit approval before writing any code
- After approval, apply only that fix — then stop again before moving to the next
- If a fix interacts with a later finding, name the dependency and ask whether to handle them together
- If the user rejects a fix, record it as explicitly deferred and move on — do not re-argue it
- Never batch fixes, even when they seem trivial
- Never assume approval carries over to the next finding

## Approval checkpoint format

Use this before every fix:

```
── Finding [N/Total] ──────────────────────────────
[SEVERITY] Short title
- Where: <file / function / config key>
- Impact: <what an attacker could do>
- Proposed fix: <what will change>
- Trade-off (if any): <what this affects beyond the vulnerability>

Proceed with this fix? (yes / skip / stop)
```

After approval, show the corrected code, then the post-fix summary:

```
✓ Applied: [SEVERITY] Short title
- Why it's safer: <one sentence>

─ Next up: [SEVERITY] Next title — ready when you are.
```

## When to use

- High-stakes codebases where every change needs a second set of eyes before it lands
- When the audit surfaced findings with significant trade-offs that need case-by-case judgment
- Onboarding a developer to security remediation — the pause forces understanding before action
- When fixes touch auth, cryptography, or data access and a silent mistake would be costly

## When not to use

- When speed matters more than control — use `security.generate` instead
- For low-risk, clearly mechanical fixes across many files (the overhead isn't worth it)
- Without a prior `security.audit` pass